
# ── Journey 5: Active Listening & Empathy ───────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':41,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Listening Without Interrupting',
'scenario':'A close friend, Lena, has asked to talk. She has been going through a difficult period with her relationship and she needs someone who will genuinely listen, not problem-solve or talk over her. You know you have a tendency to jump in with solutions or finish people\'s sentences. Today you want to give her the gift of being truly heard.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Lena begins: "I don\'t know, things with Dan have just felt really off lately. It\'s like we\'re talking but not actually communicating..." She pauses. What do you do?',
'Hold the silence for a beat, give her an encouraging nod, and gently say, "Tell me more about that."',
['Jump in: "Yeah, communication is so key — have you tried couples therapy?"','Finish her thought: "...like you\'re roommates rather than partners?"','Tell her about a time you had a similar experience with your own relationship.'],
'Holding silence and inviting her to continue — without filling the pause yourself — is the single most powerful listening act you can do. It signals that her words have weight and that you are in no hurry.',
'Jumping in with couples therapy before she has even finished her first sentence is problem-solving before she has had a chance to define the problem. Finishing her thought, even accurately, removes her authorship of her own story. Sharing your own experience redirects the conversation to you.'),
mcq(2,'Lena continues: "It\'s like every time I try to bring something up, he just gets defensive. And then I feel like I have to manage his emotions on top of my own." She looks at you expectantly. What do you say?',
'"That sounds exhausting — managing your own feelings and then his reaction on top. What\'s it like when that happens?"',
['"Dan sounds really immature — you deserve better."','"I think he probably doesn\'t mean to be defensive. He might just feel attacked."','"Have you tried starting with an \'I feel\' statement? That can help a lot."'],
'Reflecting the emotional weight she just described — exhausting — and asking an open question that deepens her exploration is exactly right. You are tracking her experience, not evaluating the relationship.',
'Immediately calling Dan immature is a judgment based on one perspective that she may not share. Defending Dan\'s behaviour is a defence of someone she did not ask you to defend. An "I feel" statement tip is advice she did not ask for.'),
mcq(3,'Lena says, "And I guess I\'m not sure if this is something that will pass or if it\'s... I don\'t know. Something bigger." She trails off. How do you respond?',
'Say softly: "It sounds like you\'re carrying a lot of uncertainty right now. Is it okay to just sit with that for a minute rather than trying to figure it out right away?"',
['Say, "It\'s probably just a rough patch — every relationship goes through these."','Tell her what you think it is: "Honestly, it sounds bigger from what you\'re describing."','Quickly reassure her: "I\'m sure it will work out — you two are so good together."'],
'Naming the uncertainty she is experiencing — rather than resolving it — and offering to simply be present with it is a profound act of companionship. You are not rushing her toward a conclusion.',
'"Just a rough patch" is minimising. Telling her you think it sounds bigger is your conclusion, not hers to reach. "I\'m sure it will work out" is a false reassurance that dismisses the seriousness of what she just shared.'),
mcq(4,'Lena says, "Thanks for not trying to fix it — I just needed to say it all out loud." How do you respond?',
'"I\'m really glad. Sometimes saying it out loud is what helps most. I\'m here — and whenever you want to think through any of it further, I\'m ready for that too."',
['"Of course — and here\'s what I actually think you should do..."','Start sharing your opinion on Dan and the relationship now that she has finished.','Say, "Anytime — that\'s what I\'m here for!" and quickly change the subject.'],
'Affirming the value of the speaking-out-loud process and leaving a gentle open door for future conversation — without switching to problem mode — keeps you in the listening role she needed.',
'Opening with "here\'s what you should do" the moment she finishes is taking the opportunity of her gratitude to pivot to advice. Sharing your opinion on Dan now is using her vulnerable moment to insert your evaluation. Quickly changing the subject signals the conversation made you uncomfortable.'),
mcq(5,'Lena asks, "What do you think I should do?" How do you respond responsibly to this invitation?',
'"What do I think you should do? I think you probably already know more about this than I do from the inside. What does your gut tell you?"',
['Give her a detailed action plan for what she should say to Dan on the weekend.','Say, "I told you I\'m not going to tell you what to do!"','Say, "I honestly have no idea — relationships are so complicated."'],
'Turning the question back to her — acknowledging her deeper knowledge of the situation — respects her autonomy and continues the listening rather than ending it with your prescription.',
'A detailed action plan substitutes your judgment for hers at the moment she is most ready to trust it. "I told you I\'m not going to tell you" is slightly playful but dismissive. "I have no idea" is an abdication that leaves her with nothing when she is directly asking for engagement.')],
'response':resp('You listened without interrupting, reflected emotion without judging, held space for uncertainty, affirmed the value of speaking aloud, and returned the question back to her when she asked for your opinion. Now type or say aloud the message you would send Lena the next day just to check in.',
'Beautiful! Listening without interrupting is not a passive act — it requires enormous self-restraint and genuine care. When someone feels truly heard, something shifts for them. You gave Lena that today.')})

levels.append({'id':42,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Reflecting and Paraphrasing',
'scenario':'You are in a mentoring session with a younger colleague, Sam, who is navigating whether to take a new internal role. He is clearly wrestling with the decision and is talking in circles — going back and forth between the appeal of the new opportunity and his anxiety about leaving his current team. You want to use reflecting and paraphrasing to help him get clarity.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Sam says: "The new role is exciting but I\'m worried it\'ll change my relationship with my team — I\'ve worked really hard to build trust there and I don\'t want to lose that." How do you paraphrase?',
'"So if I\'m hearing you right, the opportunity is genuinely appealing but what\'s holding you back is the fear of losing something you\'ve invested a lot in building. Is that close?"',
['"Sounds like you\'re scared to take the leap — that\'s normal."','Say, "I think what you\'re saying is you should stay in your current role."','"Yeah — change is always scary, but you\'ll probably be fine.'],
'Reflecting the two parts of his statement — the appeal and the specific fear — and checking whether you have it right gives him a mirror and keeps the door open for correction.',
'"Scared to take the leap — that\'s normal" labels his feeling rather than reflecting his actual words and slightly dismisses the specificity of his concern. Telling him you think he should stay in his current role is making a decision, not paraphrasing. "You\'ll probably be fine" is reassurance, not reflection.'),
mcq(2,'Sam says, "Exactly — it\'s taken me three years to build the kind of team culture we have now and I can\'t just walk away from that." How do you reflect back?',
'"Three years is a real investment. It sounds like the team culture means a lot to you beyond just the work — like it\'s something you\'re proud of and maybe even part of your identity there."',
['"Three years really isn\'t that long in the grand scheme of things."','Say, "You could always come back to that team eventually."','"So basically you don\'t want to leave because you\'re attached to your current team.'],
'Reflecting the significance of three years and going one layer deeper — suggesting it may connect to his identity and pride — invites Sam to confirm or refine, which helps him understand himself better.',
'"Three years isn\'t that long" directly contradicts and minimises the feeling he just expressed. "You could always come back" is a coping suggestion, not a reflection. "Basically you don\'t want to leave because you\'re attached" is accurate but slightly clinical and lacks warmth.'),
mcq(3,'Sam says: "But the new role is also a chance to develop skills I\'ll never get staying here. And I do want to grow." How do you reflect the tension?',
'"So you\'re holding two things at once: a real desire to grow and develop, and a real fear of what it might cost you. And both of those feel genuine?"',
['Say, "So what you\'re saying is you want the new role really."','Give advice: "You should always prioritise growth — that\'s how careers develop."','Say, "It sounds like you know what you want to do — you just need to give yourself permission."'],
'Naming both sides of the tension without resolving it — and asking him to confirm they both feel genuine — is excellent reflective listening. You are not pulling him toward a conclusion.',
'"So you want the new role really" collapses the tension into a single resolution he has not reached himself. "Always prioritise growth" is advice that resolves the tension with a rule. "You know what you want" assumes a conclusion he has not drawn.'),
mcq(4,'Sam pauses and says: "Yeah... both of those are real. I hadn\'t quite put it that way but that\'s exactly it." What do you say next?',
'"Good — so knowing that both are real, what would it take for the growth side to outweigh the cost side? What would need to be true?"',
['Now give Sam your opinion on what he should do.','Say, "Great — so you should probably go for the new role then."','Share a story about a time you faced a similar decision.'],
'A question that uses the clarity you just built together — both are real — to move toward his own decision-making process is the natural next step in a paraphrase-led coaching conversation.',
'Giving your opinion now uses his moment of clarity to redirect from his process to yours. "So you should probably go for the new role" misreads a moment of self-understanding as a decision. A personal story about your own experience is a pivot away from his exploration.'),
mcq(5,'Sam says: "I think I\'d need to know that the new team would value me the way my current team does. If I felt that was possible, I think I\'d go for it." What do you reflect back?',
'"So the question isn\'t really whether to grow — it\'s whether you can have the same kind of valued belonging in the new environment that you\'ve built here. That feels like something you could actually explore before you decide."',
['"Well you never know until you try!"','Say, "That\'s actually quite easy to find out — just ask the hiring manager."','"That\'s a big insight — I think you\'ve made your decision."'],
'Reflecting his insight back precisely — the question is about belonging, not growth — and gently noting it is explorable before deciding gives Sam something actionable without making the decision for him.',
'"You never know until you try" dismisses the legitimate need he just identified. "Just ask the hiring manager" is fine advice but premature when he is still forming his own thinking. "I think you\'ve made your decision" misreads self-clarity for a final commitment.')],
'response':resp('You used paraphrasing and reflecting throughout to help Sam identify his tension, clarify both sides, and arrive at his own insight. Now type or say aloud the one-question message you would send Sam the next week to help him continue this thinking without starting over.',
'Excellent! Reflecting and paraphrasing are the tools that transform a conversation from an exchange of words into a genuine act of understanding. You did not resolve Sam\'s dilemma — you helped him understand it clearly enough to resolve it himself. That is the real gift.')})

levels.append({'id':43,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Empathising Across Difference',
'scenario':'A colleague, Marcus, comes from a very different background to you — he grew up in a small rural community and is the first in his family to work in a corporate environment. He has just told you he feels like he does not belong in the company culture and that people make assumptions about him based on his accent and background. You want to listen and empathise genuinely — not just say the right things.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Marcus says: "I feel like sometimes people hear my accent and immediately expect less from me. It\'s exhausting to constantly have to prove myself." How do you respond?',
'"That sounds genuinely exhausting — carrying that extra layer of proof-building in every room, on top of just doing the work. Thank you for telling me this."',
['"I know exactly how you feel — I\'ve dealt with discrimination before too."','"That sounds really difficult — have you spoken to HR about it?"','Say, "I\'m sure most people don\'t mean it that way — they probably don\'t even notice your accent."'],
'Reflecting the specific emotional burden — the "extra layer" — and thanking him for trusting you with it is empathically accurate and creates a safe space for him to continue.',
'"I know exactly how you feel" may come from good intention but claims a shared experience you may not have had, which can feel invalidating. Directing him to HR is premature and practical before he has been heard. "They probably don\'t even notice" is minimising and contradicts his lived experience.'),
mcq(2,'Marcus says: "It\'s like I have to be twice as good just to be seen as half as credible." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s a real and painful dynamic — and it shouldn\'t be that way. I can hear how much energy that takes, even when you\'re already doing great work."',
['"That\'s not fair — I\'ll talk to the team on your behalf."','Say, "Do you think there\'s anything you could do differently to change how people perceive you?"','Explain that the company has diversity and inclusion training and things are improving.'],
'Naming the injustice honestly — "it shouldn\'t be that way" — without jumping to action or deflection, and acknowledging the cost of it, is genuine empathy.',
'Offering to speak to the team on his behalf is well-intentioned but skips asking what he wants. Asking what he could do differently implies the problem is his to solve. Pointing to diversity training implies institutional good intentions as though they address his personal experience.'),
mcq(3,'Marcus says: "I appreciate you listening. Not everyone would." How do you receive this without deflecting the acknowledgment?',
'"I\'m glad you felt comfortable telling me. I know it\'s not always easy to bring this kind of thing up, and I want you to know I take it seriously."',
['"Oh, it\'s nothing — anyone would listen in this situation."','Say, "I\'m honestly not sure I understand it fully — I can\'t really relate."','Pivot immediately to asking how you can help.'],
'Receiving the acknowledgment directly, naming the trust it requires to share this, and affirming that you take it seriously — without deflecting or minimising your role — is honest and validating.',
'"It\'s nothing — anyone would" deflects the gratitude and undervalues the act. "I\'m not sure I understand it fully" is honest but offered at the wrong moment — the moment for honest acknowledgment of your limits is before you give empathy, not after. Pivoting to help immediately suggests you have moved from listening to doing.'),
mcq(4,'Marcus says: "I\'m not sure I want to make a formal complaint — I don\'t want to become the guy who complains. I just wanted someone to know." What do you say?',
'"That makes complete sense. You came to be heard, not to start a process. Your experience is real and it matters regardless of what you decide to do with it."',
['"I think you should make the complaint — it\'s important for others too."','Say, "I understand, but it might be worth reporting it for your own protection."','"I\'ll keep this between us then — no worries.'],
'Honouring his stated intent — he came to be heard — without pushing him toward action he did not ask for is the respectful, empathic response. His choice of what to do with his own experience must remain his.',
'"You should make the complaint" imposes your judgment about what is right onto a decision that is his. "For your own protection" is still pushing a course of action he just declined. "I\'ll keep this between us — no worries" is breezy and undersells the significance of what he shared.'),
mcq(5,'As Marcus leaves, he looks lighter. How do you follow up in the days after?',
'A day or two later, send him a brief note: "I\'ve been thinking about what you shared with me the other day. I just wanted to say again that I\'m glad you told me. I\'m here if you ever want to talk more."',
['Tell your manager about the conversation to make sure it is on the radar.','Research diversity resources and send Marcus a list of links.','Do nothing — he said he just wanted someone to know, so you have done your part.'],
'A brief, warm follow-up that restates your availability — without action, advice, or escalation — honours the conversation and the trust Marcus placed in you.',
'Telling your manager without his consent is a betrayal of the trust and confidentiality he asked for. Sending links is a well-intentioned pivot to solution mode that he did not ask for. Doing nothing treats the follow-up as optional, which undervalues the relationship he extended to you.')],
'response':resp('You listened across real difference with genuine empathy, reflected his experience accurately, received his gratitude without deflecting, respected his stated preference not to escalate, and followed up warmly. Now type or say aloud the exact two-sentence message you would send Marcus two days after your conversation.',
'Remarkable! Empathising across difference is one of the deepest and most important communication acts there is. You did not pretend to have shared his experience — you honoured it. That is what makes empathy real rather than performative.')})

levels.append({'id':44,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Asking Great Questions',
'scenario':'You are in a discovery call with a potential new client, a growing mid-sized company that is considering your consulting services. You know very little about their situation and you want this call to be genuinely exploratory — to understand their real problems before you say a word about solutions. The quality of your questions will determine everything.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The client, David, says, "We\'re looking to improve our team communication — it\'s been a challenge." What is the best follow-up question?',
'"When you say communication has been a challenge — what does that actually look like day to day? Can you give me an example of a situation where it went wrong?"',
['Say, "Great — we have several communication training programmes that might fit."','Ask, "Is it more of an internal team issue or is it with clients too?"','Say, "How long has this been a problem?"'],
'Asking for a specific example of what "challenge" looks like in practice gives you concrete, usable information and shows genuine interest in their reality rather than your solution.',
'Pivoting to your programmes before understanding the problem is a premature close that can make David feel processed rather than heard. "Internal or client" is a multiple-choice question that narrows exploration before you have the full picture. "How long" is fine but does not reveal the actual nature of the problem.'),
mcq(2,'David gives an example where two teams had different understandings of a project brief and the resulting work had to be redone. How do you follow up?',
'"What do you think caused the different understandings — was it about the original brief itself, or about how it was communicated and confirmed along the way?"',
['"Sounds like a classic alignment issue — we see this all the time."','"How much did that rework cost you?"','Ask, "Who is usually responsible for sending the brief?"'],
'A question that distinguishes between two root causes — quality of the brief versus communication and confirmation — helps David think analytically rather than just recounting the event.',
'"Classic alignment issue — we see this all the time" implies you already know the solution, which reduces David\'s motivation to explain further. "How much did it cost" is useful data but shifts from understanding to justification. "Who is responsible" can feel like you are looking for someone to blame.'),
mcq(3,'David mentions that the issue seems to happen most when the leadership team is involved in briefing. What is the best question to explore this?',
'"Interesting — what do you think happens differently when leadership is involved in the briefing versus when it comes from the project team directly?"',
['Say, "It sounds like leadership might be part of the problem."','Ask, "Does leadership know about this pattern?"','Move to a different topic since this seems politically sensitive.'],
'An open, neutral question that invites David to compare two scenarios helps him articulate the specific dynamic without you characterising leadership as the problem.',
'"Leadership might be part of the problem" is a characterisation that David has not made and that could be politically sensitive coming from an outsider in the first call. "Does leadership know?" shifts focus from understanding to awareness. Moving away because it is sensitive means you miss potentially the most important piece of information.'),
mcq(4,'David says, "I\'ve been thinking about this problem for a year but haven\'t known where to start." What question deepens your understanding of the stakes?',
'"What would solving this change for you and the business? If this problem disappeared tomorrow, what would be different?"',
['"Why haven\'t you done anything about it for a year?"','Tell him you can help and offer to start with a proposal.','Ask, "What have you tried so far that hasn\'t worked?"'],
'Asking about the positive vision — what would be different — reveals what is at stake for him personally and for the business, and generates far more useful information than the inverse.',
'"Why haven\'t you done anything" can sound like a mild reproach. Offering to help and propose is a premature pivot from discovery to selling. "What have you tried" is also a useful question but exploring the gap first — what would be different — gives you the motivational landscape before the solution history.'),
mcq(5,'The call is nearing the end. You have learned a lot. How do you close with a question that plants the seed for the next conversation?',
'"Before we finish — what would need to be true for this conversation to have been genuinely worth your time?"',
['Ask, "Would you like to see a proposal?"','Say, "What\'s your budget for this kind of work?"','Summarise everything you heard and tell David what the solution is.'],
'This is a powerful, generative closing question that invites David to articulate value from his own perspective — and signals that you care about his criteria, not just a next step.',
'"Would you like a proposal?" is a close before you have confirmed the problem is well-enough understood. Asking about budget at the end of a discovery call is transactional and can feel premature. Summarising and telling him the solution is exactly what you said at the start you would not do until you understood the problem.')],
'response':resp('You asked questions that distinguished causes, explored specific examples, compared two scenarios, uncovered stakes, and closed generatively — all without prematurely offering a solution. Now type or say aloud the three questions you would open with on the second call with David, based on what you learned today.',
'Outstanding! Asking great questions is not about being clever — it is about being genuinely curious. The questions you asked today told David more about your quality as a thinking partner than any pitch could. That is what builds trust in a first conversation.')})

levels.append({'id':45,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Reading Emotional Subtext',
'scenario':'Your colleague Rachel comes to you and says, "I\'m fine — it\'s all fine. Things are just busy right now." Her voice is flat, her shoulders are hunched, and she is not making eye contact. You have known Rachel for two years and something is clearly off. You want to acknowledge the subtext of what she is communicating without being intrusive or presumptuous.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Rachel has just said, "I\'m fine — it\'s all fine." How do you respond to what you are seeing, not just what she said?',
'"You\'re saying you\'re fine, and I want to take that at face value — but you also don\'t quite look fine right now. I\'m not going to push, but I want you to know I notice and I\'m here."',
['"Okay, glad to hear it! Let me know if you need anything."','"You\'re clearly not fine — what\'s going on?"','Immediately start problem-solving by asking what is making her busy.'],
'Naming what you see without forcing her to open up — and leaving the door open — is exactly right. You acknowledge the gap between her words and her body language without making her defend herself.',
'"Glad to hear it" accepts the surface statement and closes the door that her body language just opened. "You\'re clearly not fine" is blunt and can make someone feel exposed before they are ready. Asking what is making her busy accepts the "busy" explanation without acknowledging the emotional signal.'),
mcq(2,'Rachel pauses and then says, "I\'m just tired. It\'s nothing specific." Her voice wavers slightly on "specific." How do you respond?',
'"Okay — tired I understand. And if it ever is something specific, you don\'t have to have it all figured out before you bring it to me."',
['"Okay — you should try to get more sleep."','Say, "It sounds like it is something specific, honestly."','Start telling Rachel about a time you were really tired and how you got through it.'],
'Accepting her framing — "tired, okay" — while leaving the door open with explicit permission to bring something unresolved creates safety without pressure.',
'Advice about sleep is a practical response to an emotional signal. "It sounds like it is something specific" names the subtext but can feel confrontational if she is not ready to go there. A story about your own tiredness redirects to your experience.'),
mcq(3,'Rachel looks at you for a long moment and then says, "Do you ever feel like you\'re just doing everything you\'re supposed to do and somehow it still feels wrong?" How do you respond?',
'"Yes. And I think that\'s one of the loneliest feelings — doing everything right and still feeling like something is off. It makes it hard to even name what\'s wrong."',
['Say, "That\'s just life — it gets easier."','Immediately ask what you think the cause might be.','Say, "You should talk to someone — like a professional."'],
'Affirming the experience without resolving it, and naming why it is particularly hard — difficult to name what is wrong — meets her exactly where she is.',
'"That\'s just life" is dismissive of a real and specific feeling. Asking what you think the cause is moves to diagnosis before she has finished disclosing. "Talk to a professional" is potentially good advice but premature at this moment when she has just started to open up.'),
mcq(4,'Rachel says, "Yeah. That\'s exactly it. I haven\'t been able to say that out loud before." How do you hold this moment?',
'Say quietly: "I\'m glad you could say it now. You don\'t have to figure it out right here — but you\'re not carrying it alone anymore."',
['Immediately ask follow-up questions to understand the situation better.','Say, "I\'m sure it will all make sense eventually."','Start suggesting things she might try to address the feeling.'],
'Honouring the disclosure without rushing to understand or resolve it — and offering genuine companionship — is the perfect response to a vulnerable breakthrough moment.',
'Asking follow-up questions immediately can feel like an interrogation after a moment of genuine release. "It will all make sense eventually" is a platitude that minimises the weight of the moment. Jumping to suggestions treats vulnerability as a problem to solve.'),
mcq(5,'Rachel smiles for the first time and says, "Thank you. I didn\'t expect to say any of that." How do you close?',
'"I didn\'t expect it either — and I\'m really glad we talked. You\'ve got my number. Whenever you want to talk more — or not talk and just not be alone — I\'m here."',
['Say, "Anytime — you know I\'m always here!" and quickly get back to your work.','Now share what you think the underlying issue probably is.','Suggest that she speak to HR or her manager about what she is feeling.'],
'Receiving the gratitude warmly, naming the unexpected nature of the conversation, and extending a specific double offer — to talk or to just not be alone — is generous and emotionally intelligent.',
'"Anytime!" and quickly returning to work can make the conversation feel neatly concluded when Rachel may still be feeling vulnerable. Sharing your theory about the underlying issue is the wrong move at the end of a conversation that was about her finding her own words. Suggesting HR or the manager may be appropriate eventually but is premature here.')],
'response':resp('You noticed the gap between Rachel\'s words and her body language, held space for her at each stage without rushing, affirmed her when she found the words, and offered genuine companionship without solving. Now type or say aloud the message you would send Rachel two days later.',
'Beautiful! Reading emotional subtext — noticing what is not being said — is one of the deepest forms of human attunement. You did not let Rachel\'s polite deflection be the whole conversation. That is what genuine care looks like.')})

levels.append({'id':46,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Validating Without Agreeing',
'scenario':'A friend, Chris, is venting about a dispute with his landlord. From his account, it sounds like both Chris and the landlord have contributed to the situation, but Chris sees it entirely as the landlord\'s fault. He is not asking for your assessment — he is venting and wants to feel understood. You want to validate his feelings without pretending to agree with an account you think is partial.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Chris opens with: "My landlord is completely unreasonable — he refused to fix the boiler for two weeks and now he\'s threatening to keep my deposit." How do you respond?',
'"Two weeks without a working boiler — that\'s genuinely awful, especially in winter. I can see why you\'re angry."',
['"That is completely unacceptable — your landlord sounds like a nightmare."','"Have you checked your lease? You might have more rights than you think."','Ask immediately, "What did you say to him when he refused the first time?"'],
'Validating the specific hardship — two cold weeks — and his emotional response — anger — is genuine empathy without wholesale agreement with his characterisation of the landlord.',
'"Your landlord sounds like a nightmare" adopts his framing uncritically. Asking about his lease is practical but pivots away from his emotional need before it has been met. Asking what he said the first time can feel like you are already looking for his fault.'),
mcq(2,'Chris continues: "And he has the nerve to say I damaged the shower — which is complete rubbish. It was already like that when I moved in." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s really frustrating — especially if you know you didn\'t cause it. What does your move-in inventory say about the shower?"',
['"He\'s lying — landlords do this all the time to keep deposits."','Say, "Well, it is hard to prove these things unless you have it in writing."','"I\'m sure you\'re right — you always look after your places well."'],
'Validating his frustration while asking a neutral, practical question — what does the inventory say — keeps you honest without feeling adversarial.',
'"He\'s lying" takes a position you have no basis for. "It\'s hard to prove things" feels skeptical of Chris at the wrong moment. "You always look after your places" is a blanket endorsement of his character that bypasses the specific claim.'),
mcq(3,'Chris says, "I sent him a message saying I would report him to the council and he went completely crazy." How do you respond without getting drawn into judging either side?',
'"So the conversation escalated at that point. That sounds like a really unpleasant exchange — how are you feeling about the whole situation now?"',
['"Threatening to report him was probably going to set him off — that wasn\'t the smartest move."','"Good — he needed to know you were serious."','Start recounting a similar experience you had with a previous landlord.'],
'Neutrally noting that the conversation escalated — without judging whether Chris\'s move was right or wrong — and pivoting to his emotional state keeps the focus on him.',
'"That wasn\'t the smartest move" offers a judgment he did not ask for. "Good — he needed to know" validates the tactic uncritically. Sharing your own landlord story redirects to your experience.'),
mcq(4,'Chris says: "I just want to feel like someone is on my side." How do you respond honestly?',
'"I\'m on your side — which means I want what\'s actually best for you, not just agreement. I think your frustration is completely valid. I also think the best outcome here requires a bit of strategy."',
['Fully agree with everything Chris said to make him feel supported.','Say, "I understand, but I have to be honest — I\'m not sure you\'re completely right here."','Pivot immediately to what he should do next.'],
'Being "on his side" in a genuine way — which includes wanting a good outcome for him — allows you to be honest without feeling like a betrayal. You validate his feelings and signal your loyalty without lying to him.',
'Full agreement when you do not fully agree is a form of dishonesty that will not serve him well. "I\'m not sure you\'re completely right" can feel cold at the moment he has just expressed vulnerability. Pivoting straight to strategy before acknowledging his need for support misses the moment.'),
mcq(5,'Chris says, "Okay — so what do I do?" How do you give honest advice that respects what you have heard?',
'"I think the most powerful move is the most practical one: pull out the move-in inventory, photograph the shower, and write a calm, factual letter setting out the timeline. You want the council on your side — and that means giving them facts, not frustration."',
['"Just hold firm and don\'t let him bully you."','"Honestly, you might have to accept losing some of the deposit."','Tell Chris to get a solicitor immediately.'],
'Practical, specific, actionable advice — that directly serves his stated goal of a good outcome — is exactly what he needs now that he has been heard.',
'"Hold firm" is encouragement without direction. "You might have to accept losing some" is realistic but premature when Chris has not yet taken any formal steps. Immediately suggesting a solicitor is an escalation that may not be proportionate to the situation as described.')],
'response':resp('You validated Chris\'s frustration without wholesale agreement, asked neutral clarifying questions, were honest about being on his side in a real way, and gave him practical specific advice when he was ready for it. Now type or say aloud the factual letter to the landlord that Chris might send to begin a formal resolution process.',
'Excellent! Validating without agreeing is one of the most sophisticated empathy skills there is. It requires you to care genuinely about the person while maintaining your own integrity. You did both.')})

levels.append({'id':47,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Empathy in Difficult News',
'scenario':'You are a team manager and you need to tell a valued team member, Priya, that she was not selected for a promotion she applied for and clearly wanted. The committee\'s decision had nothing to do with her performance — it was a structural decision to reduce the grade level at that function. You want to deliver this news with care and ensure she feels valued and informed.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have asked Priya to come into your office. She sits down looking hopeful. How do you open?',
'"Priya, I want to talk to you about the promotion decision. I\'m sorry — you weren\'t selected. I want to tell you why and then make sure you have everything you need. Can we talk through it together?"',
['Say, "So — unfortunately we have to have a difficult conversation today."','Start with five minutes of general praise before mentioning the decision.','Send Priya an email instead to give her time to process privately.'],
'Being direct and compassionate at the same time — naming the decision immediately and then offering the explanation and support — is the most respectful way to deliver difficult news.',
'"We have to have a difficult conversation" creates dread before you have said anything. Starting with extensive praise before the news makes the praise feel like a prelude to a blow. Email is impersonal for significant news and removes the opportunity for immediate support.'),
mcq(2,'Priya looks shocked and says, "Oh. I... okay. Why?" How do you explain the reason clearly and compassionately?',
'"The committee made a structural decision to not add that grade level in this function — it has nothing to do with your performance, which has been excellent. This was a structural call, not a reflection of you."',
['"These decisions are always very difficult and involve many factors."','Say, "You were up against strong competition — it just didn\'t go your way."','Say, "I actually pushed for you — but I was outvoted."'],
'A clear, specific explanation that explicitly separates her performance from the decision — and repeats it — is the most important thing you can say in this moment.',
'"Many factors" is vague and can feel evasive at the moment she most needs clarity. "Strong competition" misrepresents the actual reason and makes it sound like she lost a contest. "I pushed for you but was outvoted" involves you in the outcome in a way that may feel disloyal to the process and is probably irrelevant.'),
mcq(3,'Priya\'s eyes fill with tears and she says, "I thought I had done everything right." How do you respond?',
'Say quietly: "You did. That\'s what makes this so hard and so unfair to you. I want to acknowledge that."',
['"Don\'t worry — there will be other opportunities."','"I know — but you can\'t always control these decisions from above."','"Try not to be too upset — it really isn\'t a reflection of you."'],
'Affirming that she did do everything right, naming the unfairness explicitly, and staying present with her in the difficulty is the empathic response this moment requires.',
'"There will be other opportunities" is true but premature — she does not need the future right now, she needs to feel heard in this moment. "You can\'t control decisions from above" is true but feels like a deflection of the pain. "Try not to be too upset" tells her how to feel.'),
mcq(4,'After a pause, Priya asks: "What does this mean for my career here?" How do you answer in a way that is honest and forward-looking?',
'"I want to be honest with you: the grade level isn\'t available in this function right now. But you have a strong profile and I want to actively support you in exploring what the right next step is — whether that\'s here or elsewhere. That is a genuine offer."',
['Say, "You\'re a valued part of the team — we really hope you\'ll stay."','Tell her there will definitely be another opportunity in the next cycle.','"Honestly, it might be worth looking externally."'],
'Honest about the current structural limitation, clear about your specific commitment to support her, and open about "whether that\'s here or elsewhere" shows she is a person you are genuinely invested in — not just a retention problem.',
'"We hope you\'ll stay" is about your needs, not hers. Promising another opportunity in the next cycle is a commitment you may not be in a position to make. Telling her to look externally without offering internal support first is abrupt and may feel like a dismissal.'),
mcq(5,'Priya thanks you and says, "I need a day to process this." How do you close?',
'"Of course — take the time you need. I\'ll check in with you Thursday and we can talk about next steps when you\'re ready. My door is open between now and then too."',
['"Take as long as you need — come back whenever."','Call Priya into a meeting the next morning to discuss her development plan.','Send Priya a development resource pack by email before she gets home.'],
'Giving her the specific time she asked for, committing to a defined check-in, and leaving an open door between now and then provides structure without pressure.',
'"Take as long as you need" is generous in spirit but lacks the structure that will help her know when to re-engage. Calling her in the next morning is too soon given her explicit need for a day. Sending resources by email before she gets home is practical but intrusive into the processing time she just requested.')],
'response':resp('You delivered difficult news directly and compassionately, gave a clear and specific reason, stayed present when she became emotional, were honest about the structural reality, and gave her the space she asked for while committing to follow up. Now type or say aloud the message you would send Priya on Thursday as you promised.',
'Remarkable! Delivering difficult news with empathy requires you to hold two things at once: the reality of what is true and the care of how it lands. You did not soften the truth — you delivered it with genuine humanity. That is leadership.')})

levels.append({'id':48,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Listening Under Pressure',
'scenario':'You are in the middle of a crisis at work — a system outage is affecting clients and your team is scrambling. In the middle of this, a client calls and is extremely upset. They are not asking for a technical explanation right now — they are angry and they need to be heard first. Your instinct is to go straight to the fix, but you know you need to listen first.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The client, David, says: "This is unacceptable — we have three hundred users locked out and our operations are at a complete standstill. Do you have any idea what this is costing us?" How do you open?',
'"David — I hear you, and I\'m taking this very seriously. Before I give you a technical update, I want to acknowledge that what you\'re experiencing right now is completely unacceptable. I\'m sorry."',
['"David — our team is working on it. We should have an update in thirty minutes."','Say, "I understand your frustration — can you tell me what system you\'re experiencing issues with?"','Say, "I know, I know — we\'re fully aware. It\'s a priority."'],
'Naming that you hear him, acknowledging the unacceptability explicitly, and apologising before any technical update signals that you are responding to him as a human being, not just a ticket.',
'Going straight to the technical update skips the acknowledgment David needs. Asking him to describe the system he is experiencing issues with — when you know about the outage — can feel obtuse and dismissive. "I know, I know" is flippant and undersells the seriousness.'),
mcq(2,'David says: "Sorry is not enough — this is the third outage in four months. We have a contract and you are not meeting it." How do you respond under the pressure?',
'"You\'re right — you have a contract and we have not met it. That is a legitimate grievance and I\'m not going to deflect from it. We need to talk about it seriously once we have you back up."',
['"I understand, but our team has been working really hard on stability."','Say, "We should look at the contract terms together."','Say, "I\'ll flag it to my manager and we\'ll be in touch."'],
'Acknowledging the specific, legitimate grievance — a contract not being met — without defending your team or deflecting, and committing to address it properly once the immediate crisis is resolved is exactly right.',
'"Our team has been working really hard" is a defensive response to a legitimate complaint. "Let\'s look at the contract terms" sounds litigious. "I\'ll flag it to my manager" passes the conversation up the chain before David has felt heard.'),
mcq(3,'David is still angry: "I need to know what you are going to do about this right now." How do you respond with both empathy and clarity?',
'"Right now: I\'m getting my technical lead on a bridge call with you in the next five minutes with a live status update. And I\'m personally on this call with you until you\'re back online."',
['"We\'re working on it — that\'s all I can tell you right now."','Give a long technical explanation of what is causing the outage.','Say, "I\'ll call you back as soon as we have more information."'],
'Giving two specific, immediate, personal commitments — the bridge call and staying on the line yourself — turns empathy into tangible action that David can see and feel.',
'"That\'s all I can tell you" is transparent but leaves David without any visible progress. A long technical explanation is the wrong thing at the wrong moment — he is not asking for the cause, he is asking for action. "I\'ll call you back" delays the connection he needs right now.'),
mcq(4,'The technical lead joins and the situation is improving. David sounds slightly calmer. How do you acknowledge this shift without premature celebration?',
'"David — it sounds like things are moving. I want to stay right here with you until we confirm full restoration. We can talk about next steps once you\'re back up and the dust settles."',
['Say, "Great news! Looks like we\'ve got it sorted!"','Start explaining what caused the outage now that David sounds calmer.','Step back and let the technical lead handle it from here.'],
'Staying present, confirming you will not leave until full restoration, and deferring the broader conversation until the crisis is genuinely over shows committed accountability.',
'"Looks like we\'ve got it sorted!" is premature celebration before restoration is confirmed. Starting the technical explanation now misreads David\'s calmer state as readiness for detail. Stepping back at this point, after committing to stay, would break your word.'),
mcq(5,'Service is restored. David says, "Okay. We\'re back up. I\'m still not happy — but thank you for staying on the line." How do you close this call?',
'"I\'m glad you\'re back up. I\'m not going to ask you to be happy about this — it was unacceptable. I\'ll call you tomorrow morning with a full incident report and a proposal for how we address the contract and stability concerns together. Is 9am okay?"',
['Say, "Great — I\'m really glad we got there in the end! Talk soon."','Ask David to complete a satisfaction survey about the call.','Tell David your manager will contact him about the contract issues.'],
'Closing with honest acknowledgment that the situation was unacceptable, a specific tomorrow-morning commitment, and a specific agenda for that call is professional, accountable, and forward-looking.',
'"Really glad we got there!" is tone-deaf after an operational crisis and a legitimate complaint about contract performance. A satisfaction survey after a crisis call is both insensitive and premature. Redirecting the contract conversation to your manager passes on what you personally committed to address.')],
'response':resp('You listened under intense pressure, acknowledged the legitimate grievance directly, gave specific personal commitments, stayed through restoration, and closed with a specific accountable follow-up. Now type or say aloud the incident report email you would send David tomorrow at 9am.',
'Outstanding! Listening under pressure — when every instinct pushes toward action — is one of the most demanding and most important communication skills a professional can develop. You showed that empathy and urgency are not opposites. You can hold both.')})

levels.append({'id':49,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Supporting Without Rescuing',
'scenario':'Your younger sister, Maya, calls you in tears. She has just had a brutal day — her manager criticised her work in front of the team, she missed a deadline, and she feels like she is failing at her job. She is catastrophising. She has a history of seeking reassurance and, if you just tell her everything will be fine, she will feel temporarily better but nothing will change. You want to support her without rescuing her from her own process.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Maya is crying and says, "I just can\'t do anything right. I\'m such a failure." How do you open?',
'"Hey — I\'m here. You\'re having a really hard day. Take a breath and tell me what happened."',
['"You\'re not a failure — you\'re great at your job!"','Say, "Stop saying that — you\'re being ridiculous."','Immediately start problem-solving: "Tell me what happened and let\'s figure out what to do."'],
'Presence, acknowledgment of the difficulty of the day, and an invitation to tell you more — not an argument against "failure" — is the right opening for someone in distress.',
'Immediate reassurance sounds supportive but skips past her experience and sets up a dynamic where she will need more reassurance each time. "You\'re being ridiculous" invalidates her feeling. Immediate problem-solving skips the emotional layer entirely.'),
mcq(2,'Maya tells you the story. Her manager criticised her work in front of the team. How do you respond to that specific element?',
'"Having your work criticised in front of your colleagues is genuinely humiliating. No wonder you\'re upset — that\'s a hard experience regardless of the actual feedback."',
['Say, "Your manager sounds awful — what a bully."','Reassure her: "I\'m sure your manager didn\'t mean it badly."','Say, "What did your colleagues think?"'],
'Naming the specific experience — public criticism is genuinely humiliating — validates her response without judging the manager on limited information.',
'"Your manager sounds awful" is a judgment of someone you have only heard one side of. "I\'m sure they didn\'t mean it badly" is a defence of the manager before Maya has had the chance to process. "What did your colleagues think?" redirects the focus.'),
mcq(3,'Maya says, "I just want you to tell me it\'s going to be okay." How do you handle this request honestly?',
'"I could tell you that — and in some sense I believe it. But I know from experience that me saying it doesn\'t help for long. What I think would help more is talking through what actually happened and what it means."',
['"Of course it\'s going to be okay! You always land on your feet."','Say, "I\'m not going to tell you that — I don\'t want to give you false hope."','Give her the reassurance and move on: "It\'s going to be fine, I promise."'],
'Being honest about why you are not going to give the simple reassurance — it does not help for long — and offering something more useful is gentle, honest, and loving.',
'Immediate reassurance is temporarily comforting but does not address the underlying anxiety. "I\'m not going to tell you that" is too blunt and can feel withholding. "I promise" is a promise you cannot actually keep.'),
mcq(4,'Maya says, "Okay — what do you think I should actually do?" How do you help her without solving it for her?',
'"What do you think is the most important thing to address first? The missed deadline, the public criticism, or something else?"',
['"Here\'s what I would do: first, email your manager tonight to apologise..."','Say, "I don\'t know — it\'s your situation."','Suggest she quit the job since the manager is clearly problematic.'],
'Returning the question to her with a structured version that helps her prioritise from her own knowledge of the situation is supporting without rescuing.',
'A detailed action plan from you solves the problem but removes her agency and positions her as someone who cannot figure things out herself. "I don\'t know — it\'s your situation" is a bit cold. Suggesting she quit based on one account of one bad day is a significant overcorrection.'),
mcq(5,'Maya says, "Okay — I think I need to talk to my manager. I\'m scared but I think that\'s the right move." How do you close?',
'"I think that sounds right. And I\'m proud of you for getting there yourself. Come back and tell me how it goes — I\'m rooting for you."',
['Immediately offer to help her script what she will say to the manager.','Offer to call the manager on her behalf.','Say, "Yes! You should totally do that — here\'s what to say..."'],
'Affirming her decision, naming that she got there herself, and expressing genuine support without taking over is the perfect close.',
'Immediately scripting what she should say — even helpfully — subtly signals that you do not quite trust her to do it herself. Calling on her behalf is a complete rescue that removes her from her own situation. "Here\'s what to say" is the same as scripting but less structured.')],
'response':resp('You were present without rescuing, validated specific experiences, declined simple reassurance honestly and lovingly, returned the question to her in a structured way, and affirmed her when she reached her own decision. Now type or say aloud the message you would send Maya the morning of her conversation with her manager.',
'Beautiful! Supporting without rescuing is one of the most loving things you can do. It says: I believe in you enough not to remove the hard thing. That is real support.')})

levels.append({'id':50,'journey_id':5,'journey_title':'Active Listening & Empathy','title':'Connecting in Brief Moments',
'scenario':'You have a habit of rushing through casual interactions — hallway conversations, coffee moments, brief exchanges with colleagues you see every day. You know intellectually that these micro-connections matter, but in practice you are often distracted or in a hurry. Today you have decided to practise the skill of being genuinely present in brief moments.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'In the hallway, a colleague named Tom says, "Morning — how\'s things?" You have two minutes before a meeting. What do you do?',
'Stop walking, make brief eye contact, and say, "Good thanks — Tom, how are you actually going? You\'ve seemed a bit flat lately." and give him your full attention for ninety seconds.',
['Say, "Great, great, you?" while continuing to walk.','Say, "Crazy busy — no time, catch you later!" and keep moving.','Stop and tell Tom in detail about your morning.'],
'Stopping physically, making eye contact, asking a specific, noticing question — "you\'ve seemed a bit flat" — and committing ninety seconds of real attention turns a corridor exchange into a genuine human moment.',
'"Great, great, you?" while walking is a social script, not a connection. "No time, catch you later" is honest about your schedule but closes the door on someone who may have needed the moment. Telling Tom in detail about your own morning makes it about you.'),
mcq(2,'Tom says, "Actually — yeah. It\'s been a tough week." He looks slightly relieved that you noticed. What do you say?',
'"I\'m glad I asked. I\'ve got two minutes before my meeting — that\'s not enough to do this justice. Can we grab five minutes at lunch?"',
['Say, "Oh no! What\'s going on? Tell me everything."','Say, "Sorry — I really do have to run. But hope it gets better!"','Nod sympathetically and keep walking toward your meeting.'],
'Naming the limit of your time honestly and immediately offering a real alternative — lunch — turns a brief moment into a kept promise rather than a dropped thread.',
'"Tell me everything" before a meeting you need to attend is an offer you cannot deliver on. "Hope it gets better" and walking away closes the door after he opened it. Nodding and walking away is the same thing with less words.'),
mcq(3,'At lunch, Tom joins you. He shares briefly that he is struggling with a personal matter. You have forty minutes. How do you open the real conversation?',
'"Thanks for taking me up on lunch. No agenda from me — just here. What\'s going on?"',
['Jump straight into giving Tom advice based on what he said in the hallway.','Start with a long update on your own week first to make him comfortable.','Say, "Right — so tell me all about this tough week" with a sense of urgency.'],
'A simple, open, unhurried invitation — "no agenda, just here" — creates the space Tom needs without pressure or performance.',
'Advice before he has fully explained is presumptuous. Leading with your own week shifts the focus to you at the moment Tom needs it on him. "Tell me all about it" with a sense of urgency makes the conversation feel like an extraction.'),
mcq(4,'Tom shares something genuinely difficult. Lunch is winding up with ten minutes left. How do you handle the time pressure?',
'"Tom — I want to keep talking about this and ten minutes isn\'t enough. I\'m going to block time Thursday afternoon — can we continue then? I don\'t want to rush this."',
['"Okay — so we have to wrap up soon. What do you think you\'ll do about it?"','Say, "Anyway — I\'m sure it\'ll work out. You\'re a resilient person."','Let the lunch run over your next commitment without telling Tom.'],
'Explicitly refusing to rush the conversation and blocking future time is the most generous and honest thing you can do when time becomes a real constraint.',
'"What do you think you\'ll do about it?" is a sudden pivot to problem-solving under time pressure, which is jarring. "I\'m sure it\'ll work out" is a platitude that closes the conversation prematurely. Silently running over your next commitment is dishonest about your availability.'),
mcq(5,'On Thursday, Tom arrives for the follow-up and says, "I wasn\'t sure you\'d actually remember to block the time." How do you respond?',
'"Of course — this mattered. And so do you."',
['"Ha — I almost forgot honestly, glad I put it in the calendar."','Say, "No problem at all — what are colleagues for?" and quickly pivot to the topic.','"I always follow through on things like this — it\'s just who I am."'],
'A short, direct, warm affirmation — "this mattered, and so do you" — makes the moment real. It does not need any more words than that.',
'"I almost forgot" undercuts the care that earned Tom\'s comment. "What are colleagues for" makes it generic when Tom was specifically surprised you remembered. "It\'s just who I am" is slightly boastful and turns Tom\'s appreciation into a prompt for self-flattery.')],
'response':resp('You turned a corridor greeting into a real check-in, honoured the time limit by offering a proper alternative, created unhurried space at lunch, refused to rush the conversation under time pressure, and showed up on Thursday as promised. Now type or say aloud one thing you would change about your daily routine to protect five minutes per day for this kind of genuine brief connection.',
'Superb! The briefest moments of genuine connection compound over time into the strongest professional and personal relationships you will ever have. Presence is not about duration — it is about quality of attention. You showed that today in every exchange.')})
